00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:42.050
So we build models to assist
with those things, as well.

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:46.470
Thank you.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:49.380
I know when I was looking
at the bio you sent

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:51.750
and you specifically
talked about you

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:54.240
get excited to tell
stories with data.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.060
And I like the way
that you framed that.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:00.270
I'm wondering, do you
have any tips for us

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:03.630
on how to tell better
stories with data?

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:04.320
Sure.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:10.040
So this is something that
I've actually struggled

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:11.420
with in the past, I'll say.

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:13.760
This is something
that I learned was

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:15.600
an area of improvement for me.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.450
So over the last few years, I've
really focused on this area.

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:21.630
So definitely I can
tell you what I know.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:27.680
In my mind, data science is as
much a science as it is an art.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:29.930
To be able to tell
your story, you

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:32.060
could write hundreds
of lines of code

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:34.603
and you could build
amazing models,

00:06:34.603 --> 00:06:36.770
but at the end of the day,
if you can't translate it

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:41.350
to a non data person as a data
story, all that is a waste.

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:41.850
Right?

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:43.840
You're not adding any value.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:47.020
Businesses don't
care about your code.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:50.140
My boss doesn't care about
my hundreds of lines of code.

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:52.340
He wants value.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:58.150
So I call it the
art of data stories.

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:00.460
It's telling your data
story that's an art.

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:04.600
So one thing I started doing,
and actually I took a course

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:08.890
by Kevin Hartman from
Google, which talks about how

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:10.930
data storytelling is an art.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:12.760
And one thing I learned
from that course

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:17.830
is you want to start with
a solution for the problem

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:20.950
in a tool agnostic environment.

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:23.350
Don't start with your
tool when the problem

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:24.520
is posed in front of you.

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:28.210
Because what happens is the tool
is going to limit your ability

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:29.830
to tell your story.

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:31.930
If I say I'm going
to use Power BI,

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:34.420
then I'm going to be
limited to the visuals that

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:36.460
are available in Power BI.

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:37.150
Right?

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:41.710
So what I started
doing, literally,

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:44.480
blank page and a pen.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:47.070
What do I want my
outcome to look like?

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:50.330
And once I know
what that story is

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:52.790
going to look like on
a blank sheet of paper,

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:56.120
then I decide what
tool I'm going to use.

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:58.850
Now the tool is going to
depend on how quickly do I

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:00.330
need to turn this around?

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:02.090
How complex is this task?

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:05.150
And how good am
I with this tool?

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:05.650
Right?

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:07.483
So those are the things
you need to look at.

00:08:07.483 --> 00:08:11.110
So everyone's, oh,
Python's better than R,

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:12.130
R is better than Python.

00:08:12.130 --> 00:08:13.458
No, Power BI is the way to go.

00:08:13.458 --> 00:08:14.500
Tableau is the way to go.

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:17.170
No, everybody's right
and everybody is wrong.

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:19.540
Your tool of choice will
depend on the problem

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:20.840
you're trying to solve.

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:22.540
So that is what
I'm going to say.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:27.010
I really love
that perspective.

00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:30.050
I see a lot of comments
in the chat, too.

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:31.750
I thought about that
before too, even

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:33.309
when you're making
presentations,

00:08:33.309 --> 00:08:36.159
like sometimes if you jump
straight into the slides,

00:08:36.159 --> 00:08:37.900
you become attached to them.

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:40.960
Don't want to give them up
even if it wasn't the right way

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:42.152
to communicate it.

00:08:42.152 --> 00:08:43.360
Yeah, and that's the thing.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:45.970
Don't fall in love with
anything you're doing.

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:47.110
Don't fall in love with it.

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:49.270
Because that's what
the data world is.

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:51.850
Today you're doing
it one way, you're

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:54.430
going to find a better way to
do it as you keep learning.

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:57.960
There's always a better way.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:01.050
Priyanka, I see you had asked
in the chat about that course,

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:04.470
and I was trying to find it
at the same time, as well.

00:09:04.470 --> 00:09:07.260
Namrata, do you know what
the name of that was?

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:08.190
Yes.

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:10.270
Just give me one
second to look for it.

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:14.190
It was Business Analytics,
Communicating With Data

00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:17.110
by Kevin Hartman.

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:18.910
And I did it on Coursera.

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:21.770
But it might be
offered elsewhere, too.

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:24.850
So I wouldn't limit
myself to Coursera.

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:25.750
OK.

00:09:25.750 --> 00:09:26.282
Thank you.

00:09:26.282 --> 00:09:27.990
Marlene, I see you
have your hand raised.

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:28.948
Do you want to jump in?

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:31.780
Yeah.

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:32.280
Hi.

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:32.950
Good morning.

00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:34.730
Or afternoon, I don't
know where everybody's at,

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:35.688
so it could be evening.

00:09:35.688 --> 00:09:37.300
If you're 8 hours
ahead, I apologize.

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:39.720
Hello to whatever day you're in.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:40.600
Namrata, thank you.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.750
I mean, I'm already digging
all the things you're saying.

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:47.920
Much love to just throwing
the box away and starting

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:50.290
with a new box instead of
thinking outside the box,

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:52.450
in the box, et cetera.

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:55.330
So my question is
kind of targeted.

00:09:55.330 --> 00:09:56.997
And I don't want you
to feel like you're

00:09:56.997 --> 00:09:58.788
giving up trade secrets,
because that's not

00:09:58.788 --> 00:09:59.770
what I'm probing for.

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:03.490
But so my mom used to work
for UPS in loss prevention

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:04.300
for many moons.

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:07.250
Like she was there for
I think 28 odd years.

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:07.750
Wow.

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:10.360
And it's really cool,
though, because she

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:11.620
was kind of a pioneer.

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:13.840
I have to say that, I think
it's one of the qualities

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:16.600
that I hope I inherited from
her, or she's a bit of a sleuth

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:20.770
and wants to provide
solutions to problems.

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:24.840
So one thing she did though,
this was back on pen and paper,

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:29.310
where she used to do
credit card fraud tracking.

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:33.120
So she would take known
credit card fraud addresses is

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:34.435
what she called her.

00:10:34.435 --> 00:10:35.310
Called it, excuse me.

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:38.580
Where some, she used to
work in the downtown La hub

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:39.690
here in Los Angeles.

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:41.550
And she used to say,
OK, there's this one

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:44.370
address in the middle of like
a not so good neighborhood.

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:47.310
And there are five credit cards
that are all sending stuff

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:48.660
to the same address.

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:50.770
And she would do this by hand.

00:10:50.770 --> 00:10:54.690
And then eventually when I
was very early on in my data

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:57.730
analytics career, I built
her a little database

00:10:57.730 --> 00:11:00.270
so she could easily look
stuff up and see duplicates

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:02.140
for a single address,
things like that.

00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:05.790
And it wound up saving
like millions of dollars.

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:08.340
I didn't realize until at
the end of the year one year.

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:10.290
Her boss came and
said, do you realize

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:11.995
we got a letter from
Disney that said

00:11:11.995 --> 00:11:13.370
that you've saved
them $5 million

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:15.420
this year because of all like.

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:20.310
People would send, for example,
tickets to Disneyland in boxes

00:11:20.310 --> 00:11:23.190
to these addresses bought
on stolen credit cards

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:24.660
or fraudulent credit cards.

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:28.815
And for those of you familiar
with southern California,

00:11:28.815 --> 00:11:29.940
we have Knott's Berry Farm.

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:33.870
Around October, we
have not Scary Farm,

00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:38.220
which is a really big
event for a theme park.

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:40.440
Also hundreds of
thousands of dollars

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:43.800
she would save them from
fraudulent tickets being sold

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:45.953
and purchased on credit cards.

00:11:45.953 --> 00:11:47.370
So what I'm getting
to, I'm sorry,

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:48.495
it's a very long backstory.

00:11:48.495 --> 00:11:51.330
I feel the same way too,
it's like an epic back

00:11:51.330 --> 00:11:53.700
story and then a punch line.

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:57.660
How has maybe the
analytics for that,

00:11:57.660 --> 00:11:59.483
have you started
to see that maybe

00:11:59.483 --> 00:12:00.900
make their way to
your team or you

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:04.470
can maybe on a national
scale or even a global scale

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:06.780
kind of understand
the fraud part of it?

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:10.560
And/or, because it's easy
to do just like sales

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:11.790
profit loss that way.

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:15.280
But I found credit card fraud
really cool in that respect,

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:19.050
because it's just a little
niche pocket of saved revenue,

00:12:19.050 --> 00:12:19.550
we'll say.

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:22.080
Or loss overhead
prevention, right?

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:24.000
So anyway, that's
kind of my question.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.370
Yes that sounds very cool.

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:29.280
That sounds a very cool
project you worked on.

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:32.700
And your mom worked on.

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:36.450
So the UPS store is
a subsidiary of UPS.

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:39.660
So I'm not as involved
on the UPS side of things

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:41.702
as I am on the store
side of things.

00:12:41.702 --> 00:12:42.660
Although, I'm learning.

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:45.610
I'm learning, I'm starting
to make that transition.

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:49.200
But yes, fraud detection is
one project that we actually

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:52.680
worked on last year, but it
was a different kind of fraud.

00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:55.680
For sensitivity
reasons, I can't share

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:57.480
the details of that project.

00:12:57.480 --> 00:13:01.920
But we did have fraudulent
behavior happening

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:04.440
in the stores, and
we were able to use

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:10.050
our point of sale database
to actually help identify.

00:13:10.050 --> 00:13:15.030
Not pinpoint the fraud activity,
but identify the likelihood

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:18.240
that hey, this seems like
something we want to go

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:19.200
and question.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:22.970
And I'm not even exaggerating.

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:27.850
So we started last year in
April is where our finance

00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:30.080
team came to my group
and said, hey, look,

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:32.090
we're seeing this behavior.

00:13:32.090 --> 00:13:32.990
What can we do?

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:34.480
So we built a
dashboard that does,

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:36.800
it gives them all
this information.

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:40.270
You could see on
an outlier graph

00:13:40.270 --> 00:13:46.060
the fraud activity for April,
for April and March was

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:47.050
a huge spike.

00:13:47.050 --> 00:13:50.990
And there were spikes even
rolling 12-months before that.

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:53.170
So it's been
happening for a while.

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:56.500
Once we addressed it with
this report, two months

00:13:56.500 --> 00:14:01.250
later up until now, it's
like a straight line.

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:05.740
So whatever action was
driven by that data,

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:08.890
helped us kind of
mistake proof the system,

00:14:08.890 --> 00:14:11.410
such that we don't allow
that kind of fraud anymore.

00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:13.310
We kind of got ahead of it.

00:14:13.310 --> 00:14:16.030
So I think that was one of
the cool projects we worked on

00:14:16.030 --> 00:14:17.590
for sure.

00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:18.310
That's so rad.

00:14:18.310 --> 00:14:19.685
Thanks for sharing
and putting up

00:14:19.685 --> 00:14:20.920
with my very long back story.

00:14:20.920 --> 00:14:21.890
Oh no, I loved it.

00:14:21.890 --> 00:14:23.380
Thank you for sharing.

00:14:23.380 --> 00:14:24.580
Yeah.

00:14:24.580 --> 00:14:26.710
I absolutely love the stories.

00:14:26.710 --> 00:14:29.780
I see Andy just said in the
chat, too, I love the learning.

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:32.860
The truth be told, I'm
here for the stories.

00:14:32.860 --> 00:14:35.670
So I see Caitlin had
asked a question earlier

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:37.420
and I'm going to read
it for you because I

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:38.830
know you're in a cafe.

00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:42.280
But the question was, what did
your journey to leadership look

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:43.870
like?

00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:46.110
Ooh, that's a cool one.

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:47.640
All right.

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:51.960
So I, as much as I
may not seem like one,

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:56.820
I am not a very people
oriented person.

00:14:56.820 --> 00:14:58.750
I happen to be an introvert.

00:14:58.750 --> 00:15:00.667
The only reason I'm
able to talk to you guys

00:15:00.667 --> 00:15:02.250
so well is because
you are on a screen

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:04.950
and not in the same
room as me, so it helps.

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:09.970
So leadership was not
something that was on my radar

00:15:09.970 --> 00:15:12.310
that I didn't think
I was good for.

00:15:12.310 --> 00:15:17.080
But I started out
as this, when I

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:21.280
joined the UPS store I started
in a low leadership level

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:24.108
position where I had two
analysts reporting to me.

00:15:24.108 --> 00:15:25.900
And I was part of the
same team I am today.

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:33.070
I think the key factor towards
me moving to the leadership

00:15:33.070 --> 00:15:37.880
role was the fact that I can
communicate my data stories.

00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:38.380
Right?

00:15:38.380 --> 00:15:40.450
In the data world, if
you want to make it

00:15:40.450 --> 00:15:43.340
to a leadership role,
even if you don't want to.

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:46.270
And if you somehow
make it there,

00:15:46.270 --> 00:15:50.740
the driving factor is
going to be your ability

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:52.630
to tell stories with your data.

00:15:52.630 --> 00:15:58.300
And two is to build a level of
confidence in the organization.

00:15:58.300 --> 00:16:02.450
Because let's face it,
you're going to come up

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:04.170
with amazing data stories.

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:10.100
And if they don't tell a story
that people want to hear,

00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:13.700
it's going to be hard to
convince them to still do what

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:15.800
the data is telling them to do.

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:17.630
If it's counterintuitive, right?

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:22.250
Because a lot of businesses have
years and years of experience.

00:16:22.250 --> 00:16:26.390
You have subject matter experts
are going to use gut feeling.

00:16:26.390 --> 00:16:31.270
Or what I like to call just
their intuition, which is right

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:33.700
sometimes, but
sometimes it's not.

00:16:33.700 --> 00:16:36.100
So I think a big
factor, at least

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:37.960
in my success and
my current role,

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:39.880
was the fact that
I was able to build

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:42.790
better confidence in our data.

00:16:42.790 --> 00:16:48.880
And the way I did that was
the first thing we did was we

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:52.450
had data sitting in so many
different databases, Excel

00:16:52.450 --> 00:16:53.860
files, what have you.

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:57.280
We started working towards
one source of the truth.

00:16:57.280 --> 00:16:58.840
Right?

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:01.053
We're still not
there five years in.

00:17:01.053 --> 00:17:01.720
I'm telling you.

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:04.180
We're still not there, and it's
a long, long journey there.

00:17:04.180 --> 00:17:06.555
But at least we're working
towards it improving your data

00:17:06.555 --> 00:17:07.810
quality.

00:17:07.810 --> 00:17:12.420
Second was, I'll
give you an example.

00:17:12.420 --> 00:17:14.730
So we have a model
that forecasts

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:17.940
store wide sales for the month.

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:20.040
That helps us understand
how much revenue we're

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:21.900
going to generate, that
helps us understand

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:25.380
how much we can spend for
projects, how we can manage

00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:26.579
our expenses for the month.

00:17:26.579 --> 00:17:27.540
Right?

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:29.570
It all trickles down.

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:34.010
So we used to send this
forecast to the accounting group

00:17:34.010 --> 00:17:36.650
to let them know, hey, we're
going to make plan or not

00:17:36.650 --> 00:17:37.500
make plan.

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:39.920
So you need to cut
back on expenses.

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:41.510
Or hey, maybe this
is a good month

00:17:41.510 --> 00:17:45.840
to have some
additional expenses.

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:49.320
Before five years ago,
the way it was done

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:54.340
was if we had a forecast, we
would always tone it down.

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:56.950
Because we were afraid what
if the models are wrong?

00:17:56.950 --> 00:17:58.570
Right?

00:17:58.570 --> 00:17:59.950
And our model wasn't great.

00:17:59.950 --> 00:18:02.380
But with the course of
time, we built a model

00:18:02.380 --> 00:18:05.830
that was so strong and
robust that eventually we

00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:06.910
just let the model.

00:18:06.910 --> 00:18:09.130
Today, I don't even tell
people it's just there.

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:10.680
It's sitting there,
people can go look at it.

00:18:10.680 --> 00:18:12.680
And we're like, oh, OK,
we're going to make plan

00:18:12.680 --> 00:18:13.420
or we're not.

00:18:13.420 --> 00:18:13.920
Right?

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:16.180
So it was a journey
to get to a point

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:18.710
where people have
confidence in the data.

00:18:18.710 --> 00:18:24.430
So if you're one of those people
who can drive improvements, who

00:18:24.430 --> 00:18:28.450
can build confidence and
share your data stories

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:31.390
and communicate it to
a non data environment,

00:18:31.390 --> 00:18:34.480
to someone who doesn't
understand anything about data,

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:37.870
then you're on the path for
leadership is how I'll say it.

00:18:37.870 --> 00:18:40.750
Of course, the
other aspect of it

00:18:40.750 --> 00:18:45.520
is managing people, which I
have learned through experience

00:18:45.520 --> 00:18:50.950
as a type-a who is striving
to get everything perfect.

00:18:50.950 --> 00:18:54.950
I do struggle with balancing
empathy with accountability.

00:18:54.950 --> 00:18:56.680
But I think
experience teaches you

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:59.500
that as long as you're open
to learning and realizing

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:01.870
that you have to
change your ways,

00:19:01.870 --> 00:19:03.830
you can't manage
everyone the same way.

00:19:03.830 --> 00:19:09.100
So the path to managing people
is just learn with experience.

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:10.125
That's how I'll say it.

00:19:10.125 --> 00:19:11.500
I hope that answers
the question.

00:19:11.500 --> 00:19:12.875
I know it was a
very long answer.

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:17.070
It's a really great advice.

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:19.010
Thank you.

00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:20.750
Alan, I had asked
a question, too.

00:19:20.750 --> 00:19:21.940
Do you want to jump in next?

00:19:25.150 --> 00:19:25.650
Yeah.

00:19:25.650 --> 00:19:28.230
I sure will.

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:30.150
So much of this really
resonates thinking

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:33.810
about what overlap between what
your team no matter is doing

00:19:33.810 --> 00:19:35.310
and what my team is doing.

00:19:35.310 --> 00:19:37.890
And with a team that's
a small like yours

00:19:37.890 --> 00:19:42.150
but has really, really broad
like sets of responsibilities,

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:45.720
I wonder what your approach
is with the team thinking

00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:49.560
about professional development
and skill building, when it's

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:51.387
really hard to say
like your work is

00:19:51.387 --> 00:19:53.970
going to be, your work is going
to look like data engineering.

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:57.210
Or your work is going to
look like data science.

00:19:57.210 --> 00:20:00.060
Balancing everybody's
expectations and career goals

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:02.760
in a small team like that
with lots of responsibilities

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:04.600
has got to be really hard.

00:20:04.600 --> 00:20:07.050
And so I wonder what
that looks like for you?

00:20:07.050 --> 00:20:09.780
That's a great question.

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:13.500
I've been in this leader
position for two years now.

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:17.580
And I am so proud of how
far this team has come.

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:20.160
I have to say, I can't
take all the credit,

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:22.410
and I won't take any credit.

00:20:22.410 --> 00:20:26.650
But I am very glad that
I have a very good team.

00:20:26.650 --> 00:20:28.300
I have a very reliable team.

00:20:28.300 --> 00:20:30.060
So I'm going to say that.

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:33.220
They're amazing, they're
awesome, I love them.

00:20:33.220 --> 00:20:35.040
So part of it is
because they're awesome.

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:35.610
Right?

00:20:35.610 --> 00:20:41.250
The second half of it is I've
been trying to focus on, look,

00:20:41.250 --> 00:20:45.330
being in data science,
managing a data science team is

00:20:45.330 --> 00:20:48.270
different than
managing a team that's

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:51.060
operations driven or a
team that's product driven.

00:20:51.060 --> 00:20:54.370
Or even a team that's a
software engineer team.

00:20:54.370 --> 00:20:54.870
Right?

00:20:54.870 --> 00:20:57.540
It's different
because problems that

00:20:57.540 --> 00:21:01.750
come to a data science team
are not always well defined.

00:21:01.750 --> 00:21:04.660
And the solution is
not a cookie cutter.

00:21:04.660 --> 00:21:07.090
There's no right answer,
there's no wrong answer.

00:21:07.090 --> 00:21:10.670
You have the research oriented
approach to what we do.

00:21:10.670 --> 00:21:14.590
So what you need is that
drive to keep learning.

00:21:14.590 --> 00:21:17.080
And not everybody
has that drive.

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:20.600
So that is something that we've
been working on as a team.

00:21:20.600 --> 00:21:21.850
So here's what we do.

00:21:21.850 --> 00:21:23.930
We have been doing.

00:21:23.930 --> 00:21:27.280
We have, my whole
team has the option

00:21:27.280 --> 00:21:30.330
of picking their
learning platform.

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:32.330
Some will pick Coursera,
some are going to pick,

00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:36.350
I want to attend a virtual
summit by Power BI.

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:38.960
Or I want to buy
this book, whatever.

00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:43.070
You tell me what your
best learning approach is

00:21:43.070 --> 00:21:44.180
and you can have it.

00:21:44.180 --> 00:21:45.230
Right?

00:21:45.230 --> 00:21:46.340
Make sure.

00:21:46.340 --> 00:21:50.960
And the team is also
encouraged to spend like block

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:52.730
off time on your calendar.

00:21:52.730 --> 00:21:55.290
X hours a week or
x hours a month,

00:21:55.290 --> 00:21:58.160
whatever you like to
focus on learning.

00:21:58.160 --> 00:21:59.870
And put it on your calendar.

00:21:59.870 --> 00:22:01.790
No one's going to bother you.

00:22:01.790 --> 00:22:02.960
Do we do it?

00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:05.270
Sometimes.

00:22:05.270 --> 00:22:07.650
They'll block it off and
then they won't do it.

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:10.020
It happens to the best of us.

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:12.530
The other thing I started
doing is every year

00:22:12.530 --> 00:22:15.965
around the middle of the year we
have kind of a week long group

00:22:15.965 --> 00:22:17.400
camp, as I call it.

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:19.430
Where we bring in a
certified, so we've

00:22:19.430 --> 00:22:22.370
been doing R I've
been focusing, my team

00:22:22.370 --> 00:22:25.040
has been focusing on R for
the last two years at least.

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:28.520
So what we had as a certified
instructor come in and just

00:22:28.520 --> 00:22:31.620
work on little
projects with the team.

00:22:31.620 --> 00:22:33.380
So we had a week
long group camp where

00:22:33.380 --> 00:22:35.960
the team was not
bothered by anyone just

00:22:35.960 --> 00:22:37.790
to focus on learning.

00:22:37.790 --> 00:22:39.830
And then the third
thing, and I think

00:22:39.830 --> 00:22:42.530
this is where it kind
of closes the gap,

00:22:42.530 --> 00:22:44.300
is you can learn
everything online.

00:22:44.300 --> 00:22:45.823
You can learn from instructors.

00:22:45.823 --> 00:22:47.990
But then when you have to
apply it in the real world

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:50.720
it's not a one to
one translation.

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:53.240
Real world data is not
normally distributed.

00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:54.890
Real world data has noise in it.

00:22:54.890 --> 00:22:56.790
It doesn't work.

00:22:56.790 --> 00:22:58.850
So what we started
doing is we started

00:22:58.850 --> 00:23:01.460
having monthly sessions.

00:23:01.460 --> 00:23:04.700
One, we started out with
best practice sessions,

00:23:04.700 --> 00:23:06.390
where one of the team
members would say,

00:23:06.390 --> 00:23:09.980
hey look, this is a project
I worked on and here's how

00:23:09.980 --> 00:23:11.430
I approach the problem.

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:14.120
So this helps someone that's
new on the team, someone

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:16.760
that's learning that
software to kind of get

00:23:16.760 --> 00:23:18.750
some exposure to it.

00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:21.080
The other thing we started
doing more recently

00:23:21.080 --> 00:23:23.480
is we said, you know, what
we're kind of maxing out

00:23:23.480 --> 00:23:24.740
on our best practice sessions.

00:23:24.740 --> 00:23:27.630
We have nothing to
share at some point.

00:23:27.630 --> 00:23:30.350
So we started doing
a micro assignment.

00:23:30.350 --> 00:23:33.930
So I have a very, I don't know
if she's on the call today.

00:23:33.930 --> 00:23:36.320
But she was going to join.

00:23:36.320 --> 00:23:39.590
My team member Liz, she's
the supervisor of the group,

00:23:39.590 --> 00:23:40.790
or part of the group.

00:23:40.790 --> 00:23:42.770
She puts together
micro assignments

00:23:42.770 --> 00:23:44.870
for the team with
real work data.

00:23:44.870 --> 00:23:47.450
Like hey, look at
these online reviews,

00:23:47.450 --> 00:23:51.020
look at this customer data,
or look at our sales data.

00:23:51.020 --> 00:23:54.260
And perform these
operations in R.

00:23:54.260 --> 00:23:57.620
Because this year our
last year our goal was R.

00:23:57.620 --> 00:23:59.990
And the team would
do those assignments.

00:23:59.990 --> 00:24:02.870
A month later, when she
gives them a new assignment,

00:24:02.870 --> 00:24:05.720
we meet to discuss how everyone
approached their assignment.

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:07.580
And you learn a lot.

00:24:07.580 --> 00:24:09.830
You learn more when
you make mistakes

00:24:09.830 --> 00:24:12.200
than when you
successfully complete

00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:13.470
an assignment in my mind.

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:18.200
So I think that helped the team
kind of get their feet wet,

00:24:18.200 --> 00:24:19.400
if you will, with R.

00:24:19.400 --> 00:24:22.310
Two years ago the
same with Power BI.

00:24:22.310 --> 00:24:22.850
Right?

00:24:22.850 --> 00:24:25.160
So two years ago, our
goal was Power BI.

00:24:25.160 --> 00:24:27.990
Now we switch to R. We'll
pick something for this year.

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:30.720
And that's how we've been
doing it for the team.

00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:32.610
I hope that answers
your question, Alan.

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:33.110
It does.

00:24:33.110 --> 00:24:33.470
It's great.

00:24:33.470 --> 00:24:33.970
Thank you.

00:24:33.970 --> 00:24:35.990
I love that idea of
micro assignments

00:24:35.990 --> 00:24:37.550
and really, really
like the emphasis

00:24:37.550 --> 00:24:40.850
on learning and kind of
letting people drive a path

00:24:40.850 --> 00:24:41.990
that they're interested in.

00:24:44.570 --> 00:24:47.360
If we come back
around to it, it'd

00:24:47.360 --> 00:24:49.790
be great to hear more about
how you manage expectations

00:24:49.790 --> 00:24:53.070
that people have of, well
I'm learning this stuff,

00:24:53.070 --> 00:24:55.350
but I'm not getting to use it.

00:24:55.350 --> 00:24:57.300
So maybe I'll plant that seed.

00:24:57.300 --> 00:24:58.800
I don't want to
keep going, I want

00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:01.650
to hear what other folks are
interested in talking about.

00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:03.180
But thank you.

00:25:03.180 --> 00:25:05.100
I will add one
other thing, Alan.

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:06.910
Actually, two other things.

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:10.110
The other thing that, because
my team does so many things,

00:25:10.110 --> 00:25:12.000
we have predictive,
we have descriptive,

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:14.580
we have data engineering, a
little bit of data engineering,

00:25:14.580 --> 00:25:16.330
not as much.

00:25:16.330 --> 00:25:21.210
We also put together like a
central kind of repository

00:25:21.210 --> 00:25:24.960
where we have reference
guides, best practices,

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:26.490
a lot of documentation.

00:25:26.490 --> 00:25:28.110
To the point where
it is someone new

00:25:28.110 --> 00:25:30.540
joins my team, all I'm
going to tell them is hey,

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:32.940
look you're going to
spend your first week just

00:25:32.940 --> 00:25:35.280
reading everything that's
on that repository.

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:37.320
So that gives-- and
then ask questions.

00:25:37.320 --> 00:25:40.230
So I think documentation,
process documentation

00:25:40.230 --> 00:25:42.310
plays a very important role.

00:25:42.310 --> 00:25:44.760
And it allows us to kind of
cover for each other, right?

00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:47.010
Because we want to
rely on processes

00:25:47.010 --> 00:25:49.260
that make our
people's lives easier.

00:25:49.260 --> 00:25:52.530
As opposed to saying, Oh hey,
Matt's out of office today

00:25:52.530 --> 00:25:55.350
so this work's not
going to get done.

00:25:55.350 --> 00:25:56.370
So that's one.

00:25:56.370 --> 00:25:59.670
And then setting
expectations is you just

00:25:59.670 --> 00:26:01.860
have to realize that
you may learn something

00:26:01.860 --> 00:26:04.680
and you may not get a chance
to apply it in the real world.

00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:08.310
Because organizations
are not always

00:26:08.310 --> 00:26:10.470
ready for machine learning.

00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:11.520
They're not ready.

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:14.880
They're not there yet to use
these fun cool things and data.

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:16.260
You still have to
clean the data,

00:26:16.260 --> 00:26:17.677
you have to do the
grunt work, you

00:26:17.677 --> 00:26:22.470
have to do the non-fun aspects
to get to the fun aspect.

00:26:22.470 --> 00:26:26.160
But don't wait for someone
to come ask you to apply it.

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:27.840
You know where the data is.

00:26:27.840 --> 00:26:29.110
You know what you've learned.

00:26:29.110 --> 00:26:30.970
If you really want to
apply it, go apply it.

00:26:30.970 --> 00:26:33.630
And if you can build something
that's useful and adds value,

00:26:33.630 --> 00:26:37.390
hey, I'll make sure the
organization uses it.

00:26:37.390 --> 00:26:39.720
So that's how I approach that.

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:40.810
That's great.

00:26:40.810 --> 00:26:41.310
I love it.

00:26:41.310 --> 00:26:42.340
Thank you.

00:26:42.340 --> 00:26:42.840
Yeah.

00:26:42.840 --> 00:26:43.735
Thank you.

00:26:43.735 --> 00:26:45.360
Andey, I see you have
your hand raised,

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:48.590
you want to jump in next?

00:26:48.590 --> 00:26:49.340
ANDEY NUNES: Sure.

00:26:49.340 --> 00:26:49.670
Thanks.

00:26:49.670 --> 00:26:50.170
Yeah.

00:26:50.170 --> 00:26:51.140
Hi.

00:26:51.140 --> 00:26:54.800
My question is so
you just mentioned

00:26:54.800 --> 00:26:58.430
that you have sort of a
landing page for folks that are

00:26:58.430 --> 00:27:01.310
onboarding that come in new.

00:27:01.310 --> 00:27:06.650
And our group where I work was
talking about putting together

00:27:06.650 --> 00:27:07.400
a Teams channel.

00:27:07.400 --> 00:27:12.290
And I'm wondering if you have
some tips on just some of the

00:27:12.290 --> 00:27:15.870
must haves for setting up a
space like that for your team.

00:27:15.870 --> 00:27:16.370
Sure.

00:27:16.370 --> 00:27:18.530
So what we use, so
our organization

00:27:18.530 --> 00:27:21.170
uses office 365 for most things.

00:27:21.170 --> 00:27:22.947
So we have an R teams group.

00:27:22.947 --> 00:27:25.280
We don't have a channel yet,
because we're a small team,

00:27:25.280 --> 00:27:27.320
we don't really need a channel.

00:27:27.320 --> 00:27:28.670
But we have a SharePoint.

00:27:28.670 --> 00:27:31.910
SharePoint is where all
my documentation sits.

00:27:31.910 --> 00:27:34.430
But it doesn't have to
be SharePoint as long

00:27:34.430 --> 00:27:38.040
as it's a collaborative platform
where everyone can access it.

00:27:38.040 --> 00:27:41.600
So I don't know if
you use Google Drive.

00:27:41.600 --> 00:27:43.280
Start with the simple platforms.

00:27:43.280 --> 00:27:46.760
We can get to a
point where we are

00:27:46.760 --> 00:27:50.510
able to use Azure DevOps
or Azure data lake or Git

00:27:50.510 --> 00:27:51.660
or what have you.

00:27:51.660 --> 00:27:54.500
But I started with
something very simple.

00:27:54.500 --> 00:27:58.280
It's Microsoft Word documents
posted on SharePoint.

00:27:58.280 --> 00:28:01.410
Eventually, today that
folder looks beautiful.

00:28:01.410 --> 00:28:03.140
It's got subfolders,
it's got everything.

00:28:03.140 --> 00:28:04.850
But when I started
it was one folder

00:28:04.850 --> 00:28:08.000
with the Word document in it.

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:08.720
That's perfect.

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:09.440
Yeah.

00:28:09.440 --> 00:28:11.900
We're also using Microsoft
Teams in SharePoint.

00:28:11.900 --> 00:28:14.810
So I think that's
where we're starting.

00:28:14.810 --> 00:28:17.100
Thanks.

00:28:17.100 --> 00:28:18.430
Sure.

00:28:18.430 --> 00:28:22.780
So as you were explaining
those micro assignments

00:28:22.780 --> 00:28:25.885
and monthly sessions and you
mentioned that week long boot

00:28:25.885 --> 00:28:26.830
camp.

00:28:26.830 --> 00:28:29.770
I realized Katherine and
I had the same question

00:28:29.770 --> 00:28:30.670
at the same time.

00:28:30.670 --> 00:28:32.590
Katherine and I were slacking.

00:28:32.590 --> 00:28:35.500
But did you have to
convince your leadership

00:28:35.500 --> 00:28:38.890
that the team should be
given a week for learning

00:28:38.890 --> 00:28:42.730
and to not be bothered
by other things?

00:28:42.730 --> 00:28:44.980
Well, not really.

00:28:44.980 --> 00:28:47.950
Again, I consider
myself very fortunate

00:28:47.950 --> 00:28:51.590
because I've had amazing
leaders and an amazing team.

00:28:51.590 --> 00:28:55.030
So I've had an environment
where I've been able to succeed.

00:28:55.030 --> 00:29:00.490
And I realize that not
everyone has that privilege.

00:29:00.490 --> 00:29:04.400
But there have been, so when
it comes to the business

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:06.630
has to go on, right
during that week.

00:29:06.630 --> 00:29:11.460
So the way I've been doing it is
I set expectations weeks ahead.

00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:15.350
So today the way people come to
us with data requests, again,

00:29:15.350 --> 00:29:17.150
I use SharePoint for that.

00:29:17.150 --> 00:29:19.040
I build up a form
where people can

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:22.970
go in and submit their requests
with all their requirements.

00:29:22.970 --> 00:29:25.440
Of course, sometimes people
don't know what they want.

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:27.900
So that's where the data
person has to tell them,

00:29:27.900 --> 00:29:30.110
hey, this is what
the data looks like.

00:29:30.110 --> 00:29:33.360
But I have a platform where they
can go submit these requests.

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:37.400
So maybe three weeks
ahead of the training,

00:29:37.400 --> 00:29:39.800
I just set expectations
with all the managers

00:29:39.800 --> 00:29:41.630
letting them know,
hey, we're going

00:29:41.630 --> 00:29:43.230
to be in training that week.

00:29:43.230 --> 00:29:45.740
So the turnaround time
for these requests

00:29:45.740 --> 00:29:47.130
is not going to be as quick.

00:29:47.130 --> 00:29:51.020
So if you have anything that you
can plan ahead for, plan ahead.

00:29:51.020 --> 00:29:55.130
And if an emergency
situation arises that week,

00:29:55.130 --> 00:29:59.120
then I would be the one that
bows out and takes care of it.

00:29:59.120 --> 00:30:01.670
Thankfully, I do have
the technical, not,

00:30:01.670 --> 00:30:04.760
I realize not all leaders
have the technical expertise

00:30:04.760 --> 00:30:06.110
to do what their teams do.

00:30:06.110 --> 00:30:11.360
But because I've kind of come
from where the team is today,

00:30:11.360 --> 00:30:13.680
I can manage most emergencies.

00:30:13.680 --> 00:30:15.350
So I just, I was
the one on call.

00:30:15.350 --> 00:30:18.230
And then I had one of my
supervisors on call with me

00:30:18.230 --> 00:30:21.260
in the event we had to step out.

00:30:21.260 --> 00:30:24.830
Two years ago, I did
have to step out a lot.

00:30:24.830 --> 00:30:27.780
This year, last year I did
not have to step out at all.

00:30:27.780 --> 00:30:31.220
So it just depends on what's
happening at work that week.

00:30:31.220 --> 00:30:34.460
But I made sure that
the analyst and the team

00:30:34.460 --> 00:30:37.155
were not disturbed.

00:30:37.155 --> 00:30:37.780
That's great.

00:30:37.780 --> 00:30:39.250
Thank you.

00:30:39.250 --> 00:30:40.420
Sure.

00:30:40.420 --> 00:30:42.070
Eric, I see you
asked a question.

00:30:42.070 --> 00:30:43.790
Do you want to jump in?

00:30:43.790 --> 00:30:44.290
Yeah.

00:30:44.290 --> 00:30:46.750
I've done great, great hearing
your perspective on these.

00:30:46.750 --> 00:30:47.900
Really appreciate it.

00:30:47.900 --> 00:30:51.430
And I really agree
with the emphasis

00:30:51.430 --> 00:30:55.750
you said on helping teams
improve the process of not

00:30:55.750 --> 00:30:58.450
just doing the technical
work but helping others

00:30:58.450 --> 00:31:00.710
on that journey, especially
with documentation,

00:31:00.710 --> 00:31:02.530
internal resources.

00:31:02.530 --> 00:31:07.010
But what I've seen is sometimes
leadership up in the chain,

00:31:07.010 --> 00:31:09.100
so to speak, don't
really see the value

00:31:09.100 --> 00:31:11.050
of that side of
the work as opposed

00:31:11.050 --> 00:31:13.660
to just getting that
technical deadline done

00:31:13.660 --> 00:31:15.740
or getting that piece.

00:31:15.740 --> 00:31:19.480
I'm curious what your take
is on getting leadership

00:31:19.480 --> 00:31:23.740
to recognize that that side
of it of helping people,

00:31:23.740 --> 00:31:26.050
as you said the glue
work, I really like that,

00:31:26.050 --> 00:31:28.480
is just as important
as everything else.

00:31:28.480 --> 00:31:30.810
Just curious your
thoughts on that.

00:31:30.810 --> 00:31:32.688
So you're right.

00:31:32.688 --> 00:31:33.230
You're right.

00:31:33.230 --> 00:31:37.070
You're absolutely right
where for the most part,

00:31:37.070 --> 00:31:38.980
people in our roles
are working day

00:31:38.980 --> 00:31:41.830
to day just putting fires out.

00:31:41.830 --> 00:31:44.560
And prioritization
on the long term

00:31:44.560 --> 00:31:47.860
is not always an option, right?

00:31:47.860 --> 00:31:50.350
The way I did it
is I just did it.

00:31:50.350 --> 00:31:52.990
I didn't go asking
for permission.

00:31:52.990 --> 00:31:56.870
I didn't say, hey look, we
should do this, I just did it.

00:31:56.870 --> 00:32:00.160
And when I did it, now
I can show the results.

00:32:00.160 --> 00:32:04.450
I can be like hey, look,
when my team members are out,

00:32:04.450 --> 00:32:08.590
the business can still continue
because we have tools in place

00:32:08.590 --> 00:32:11.050
to help to support the team.

00:32:11.050 --> 00:32:15.450
When you give me someone
new, my team, that person

00:32:15.450 --> 00:32:20.470
can learn quickly because of the
school that we've put together.

00:32:20.470 --> 00:32:27.360
So the best way to convince
leadership is just do it.

00:32:27.360 --> 00:32:29.070
And then show them the results.

00:32:29.070 --> 00:32:33.970
Because to play the devil's
advocate in the leadership's

00:32:33.970 --> 00:32:38.680
defense, they're not going
to prioritize something

00:32:38.680 --> 00:32:41.260
if they don't see
that it can add value.

00:32:41.260 --> 00:32:41.920
Right?

00:32:41.920 --> 00:32:43.750
It's all about value.

00:32:43.750 --> 00:32:47.470
And I feel like in my position,
I'm a mid-level manager,

00:32:47.470 --> 00:32:51.880
I have some level of freedom
to make some changes,

00:32:51.880 --> 00:32:54.580
to change the rules.

00:32:54.580 --> 00:32:57.040
So I just do it.

00:32:57.040 --> 00:32:58.450
I don't go seeking permission.

00:32:58.450 --> 00:32:59.200
I do it.

00:32:59.200 --> 00:33:00.950
And then if they like it, great.

00:33:00.950 --> 00:33:02.860
If they don't, I'm
fully prepared if that

00:33:02.860 --> 00:33:04.660
gets rejected it's
going to get rejected.

00:33:04.660 --> 00:33:07.150
I can't tell you how many
slides I put together

00:33:07.150 --> 00:33:12.680
with amazing ideas and maybe
a couple have been selected.

00:33:12.680 --> 00:33:13.180
Right?

00:33:13.180 --> 00:33:15.312
So you have to kind
of balance that out.

00:33:15.312 --> 00:33:16.270
You just have to do it.

00:33:16.270 --> 00:33:17.620
Be prepared for rejection.

00:33:17.620 --> 00:33:19.670
But do it, show the results.

00:33:19.670 --> 00:33:21.940
And that's the best
way to drive change.

00:33:27.150 --> 00:33:30.660
And one other thing I
would also add there, Eric.

00:33:30.660 --> 00:33:32.560
I definitely
recommend this book.

00:33:32.560 --> 00:33:34.260
It's called Subtract.

00:33:34.260 --> 00:33:38.580
Especially if any of you are
on the path to that leadership

00:33:38.580 --> 00:33:42.360
path in the data science
world, I would highly

00:33:42.360 --> 00:33:43.950
recommend the book Subtract.

00:33:43.950 --> 00:33:49.680
Because it's not always
about what you need to do.

00:33:49.680 --> 00:33:52.650
It's also about what
you need to stop.

00:33:52.650 --> 00:33:54.570
Right?

00:33:54.570 --> 00:33:57.240
That was the time my team
was building reports.

00:33:57.240 --> 00:33:59.340
Week after week,
sending them out,

00:33:59.340 --> 00:34:01.620
we're spending all this time.

00:34:01.620 --> 00:34:06.070
And we didn't know what those
reports were being used for.

00:34:06.070 --> 00:34:08.610
And again, I'm lucky because
I have good leadership.

00:34:08.610 --> 00:34:10.562
This was a recommendation.

00:34:10.562 --> 00:34:12.270
This is something I
learned from our CEO.

00:34:12.270 --> 00:34:14.918
She recommended this book to me.

00:34:14.918 --> 00:34:15.960
This is what she told me.

00:34:15.960 --> 00:34:19.380
She's like look, don't
send those reports.

00:34:19.380 --> 00:34:21.190
Just stop.

00:34:21.190 --> 00:34:22.929
And see how many
people come knocking

00:34:22.929 --> 00:34:25.360
at your door for that report.

00:34:25.360 --> 00:34:27.610
And that's exactly what I did.

00:34:27.610 --> 00:34:32.110
To the point where now I
have a policy in my team

00:34:32.110 --> 00:34:34.480
where if we think something
is not being used the way

00:34:34.480 --> 00:34:38.199
it should be, we put it on what
we call a 90-day pause list.

00:34:38.199 --> 00:34:40.790
We don't tell anybody.

00:34:40.790 --> 00:34:43.280
We put it on a 90-day pause
and see how many people

00:34:43.280 --> 00:34:45.560
come banging on our door.

00:34:45.560 --> 00:34:47.639
Sometimes they do,
we put it back up.

00:34:47.639 --> 00:34:50.270
We're like OK, sorry,
we were just testing.

00:34:50.270 --> 00:34:51.510
And sometimes they don't.

00:34:51.510 --> 00:34:53.719
And after 90 days,
I just unset it.

00:34:53.719 --> 00:34:55.620
Like we're not
doing this anymore.

00:34:55.620 --> 00:35:01.310
And I can't tell you how much
time that has freed up for us.

00:35:01.310 --> 00:35:04.670
To actually focus on
things that matter.

00:35:04.670 --> 00:35:08.740
So Subtract is just
as important as add.

00:35:08.740 --> 00:35:11.170
So that's something I
would add there, for sure.

00:35:11.170 --> 00:35:13.460
Prioritization.

00:35:13.460 --> 00:35:14.490
Absolutely love it.

00:35:14.490 --> 00:35:18.200
I'm on that journey as we speak,
seeing some legacy processes

00:35:18.200 --> 00:35:21.950
or capabilities that
frankly have stagnated.

00:35:21.950 --> 00:35:22.470
And--

00:35:22.470 --> 00:35:22.970
Exactly.

00:35:22.970 --> 00:35:26.295
Why do we keep maintaining
it when maybe we just

00:35:26.295 --> 00:35:27.170
focus somewhere else?

00:35:27.170 --> 00:35:29.450
That's excellent advice I'm
going to check that book out.

00:35:29.450 --> 00:35:30.260
Thank you so much.

00:35:30.260 --> 00:35:32.180
Yeah, sure.

00:35:32.180 --> 00:35:32.840
Thank you.

00:35:32.840 --> 00:35:37.220
I'm saving that to go buy
this right after, too.

00:35:37.220 --> 00:35:39.657
Libby, I see you
asked a question.

00:35:39.657 --> 00:35:40.490
You want to jump in?

00:35:43.670 --> 00:35:44.170
Yeah.

00:35:44.170 --> 00:35:46.283
I asked a couple, actually.

00:35:46.283 --> 00:35:47.200
My first question is--

00:35:47.200 --> 00:35:49.630
Start with whichever
one you prefer.

00:35:49.630 --> 00:35:52.040
Yeah my first question is,
how did you get into data?

00:35:52.040 --> 00:35:55.130
Which might be a longer answer.

00:35:55.130 --> 00:35:56.920
But the other one,
I was also really

00:35:56.920 --> 00:35:58.720
interested in how
you handle failure,

00:35:58.720 --> 00:36:02.170
both in yourself and
in your teammates.

00:36:02.170 --> 00:36:04.270
You talked a little bit
about that, though right.

00:36:04.270 --> 00:36:04.900
Just do it.

00:36:04.900 --> 00:36:09.150
And a small percentage of
stuff is going to stick, right?

00:36:09.150 --> 00:36:10.500
Yeah.

00:36:10.500 --> 00:36:13.500
So I'll answer the
first one later.

00:36:13.500 --> 00:36:16.080
I'll answer the second one
because it's a shorter answer.

00:36:16.080 --> 00:36:17.460
I read this.

00:36:17.460 --> 00:36:21.260
So this is how I
look at failure.

00:36:21.260 --> 00:36:25.160
Personally, if I feel it,
I'm very hard on myself.

00:36:25.160 --> 00:36:27.050
And my husband will
attest to that.

00:36:27.050 --> 00:36:29.660
He's had to talk me down a lot.

00:36:29.660 --> 00:36:32.630
So as put together as
I come across, I'm not.

00:36:32.630 --> 00:36:35.660
I can be a total mess sometimes.

00:36:35.660 --> 00:36:39.878
I'll make one tiny mistake
and I will harp on it all day.

00:36:39.878 --> 00:36:41.420
Like, oh my god,
how could I do that?

00:36:41.420 --> 00:36:45.980
If I do that to myself,
you can imagine how

00:36:45.980 --> 00:36:47.450
I do that to my team sometimes.

00:36:47.450 --> 00:36:50.930
They might miss something,
miss a deadline here and there

00:36:50.930 --> 00:36:56.300
and I can get a little
nagging about it.

00:36:56.300 --> 00:37:00.200
But because I know that I do
that, I've been consciously

00:37:00.200 --> 00:37:06.650
working on, OK, mistake has
happened, failure has occurred.

00:37:06.650 --> 00:37:10.300
Focus on what is the impact?

00:37:10.300 --> 00:37:10.930
Right?

00:37:10.930 --> 00:37:14.890
At the end of the day,
we're not saving lives.

00:37:14.890 --> 00:37:17.120
If I make a mistake,
nobody dies.

00:37:17.120 --> 00:37:17.710
Right?

00:37:17.710 --> 00:37:19.570
That's not an accident.

00:37:19.570 --> 00:37:23.230
But nobody died, so it's fine.

00:37:23.230 --> 00:37:24.790
No I'm just joking.

00:37:24.790 --> 00:37:27.010
But that said, you
have to remind yourself

00:37:27.010 --> 00:37:29.410
every day at the end of the
day, you're not saving lives.

00:37:29.410 --> 00:37:32.200
What is the impact
of this failure?

00:37:32.200 --> 00:37:35.930
And if the impact of the
failure isn't significant,

00:37:35.930 --> 00:37:37.990
then the failure is
actually a good thing

00:37:37.990 --> 00:37:39.870
because you learn from it.

00:37:39.870 --> 00:37:41.420
So I love to learn.

00:37:41.420 --> 00:37:43.250
And I have to remind
myself every time

00:37:43.250 --> 00:37:46.020
I fail that this is a
learning opportunity.

00:37:46.020 --> 00:37:47.650
It's OK.

00:37:47.650 --> 00:37:49.930
Over the course of
time, and I hope

00:37:49.930 --> 00:37:52.000
my team will attest
to this, I also

00:37:52.000 --> 00:37:54.920
start learning to do
the same with them.

00:37:54.920 --> 00:37:57.200
Two years ago when
I became manager,

00:37:57.200 --> 00:38:00.420
I was picking on
every little thing.

00:38:00.420 --> 00:38:01.080
Right?

00:38:01.080 --> 00:38:05.230
Today someone forgets
to send something out

00:38:05.230 --> 00:38:07.360
and my response is, no worry.

00:38:07.360 --> 00:38:08.630
It's OK.

00:38:08.630 --> 00:38:10.280
And I genuinely feel that.

00:38:10.280 --> 00:38:13.370
It took me a lot of
conscious mindfulness

00:38:13.370 --> 00:38:15.550
to get to this point.

00:38:15.550 --> 00:38:18.420
So unfortunately,
there's no secret to it.

00:38:18.420 --> 00:38:20.970
It's you just have to be
conscious and mindful about it

00:38:20.970 --> 00:38:23.190
and realize that
the impact of it

00:38:23.190 --> 00:38:25.870
is not as significant as you
make out to be in your head.

00:38:25.870 --> 00:38:30.480
And then failure just seems
like a learning opportunity.

00:38:30.480 --> 00:38:32.100
I hope that answers
your question.

00:38:32.100 --> 00:38:33.300
No it's great.

00:38:33.300 --> 00:38:35.070
Thank you so much.

00:38:35.070 --> 00:38:40.110
And then how I got into
the data was, all right.

00:38:40.110 --> 00:38:41.740
I'm an industrial engineer.

00:38:41.740 --> 00:38:44.190
I have a bachelor's in
production engineering.

00:38:44.190 --> 00:38:48.060
I had one statistics
course in my master's,

00:38:48.060 --> 00:38:49.620
and one multivariate course.

00:38:49.620 --> 00:38:53.280
So no, my schooling did not
lead me into the data world.

00:38:53.280 --> 00:38:57.150
I was working in logistics
as a research assistant

00:38:57.150 --> 00:39:00.120
looking for jobs when I
landed this one with UPS.

00:39:00.120 --> 00:39:07.110
So it wasn't a planned job.

00:39:07.110 --> 00:39:09.000
I was an industrial
engineer working

00:39:09.000 --> 00:39:11.790
in continuous improvement,
doing a little bit of analytics,

00:39:11.790 --> 00:39:16.560
but not as much as
I'm doing today.

00:39:16.560 --> 00:39:20.710
I ended up in this role, I
was an immigrant at the time.

00:39:20.710 --> 00:39:24.510
And I had 15 days
on my visa to find

00:39:24.510 --> 00:39:26.610
a job when I landed this job.

00:39:26.610 --> 00:39:30.810
So I was in Georgia
and I drove all the way

00:39:30.810 --> 00:39:34.500
to San Diego for this
job because I had 15 days

00:39:34.500 --> 00:39:36.850
or I had to leave the country.

00:39:36.850 --> 00:39:43.590
So I got, that was luck played
a big factor in my entrance

00:39:43.590 --> 00:39:44.580
to the data world.

00:39:44.580 --> 00:39:49.560
But once I was here, I was
like, I'm not going back.

00:39:49.560 --> 00:39:53.490
When I joined this role I
knew how to use Power BI.

00:39:53.490 --> 00:39:57.080
I knew a little
bit about Tableau.

00:39:57.080 --> 00:39:58.700
R I only used for
school projects.

00:39:58.700 --> 00:40:02.160
And Python was, I had no idea.

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:04.960
OK.

00:40:04.960 --> 00:40:06.160
I learned on the job.

00:40:06.160 --> 00:40:07.720
I focused on learning.

00:40:07.720 --> 00:40:10.990
I realized how fun
the data world can be.

00:40:10.990 --> 00:40:14.410
I had, I was very
fortunate to have

00:40:14.410 --> 00:40:18.880
a peer who was very, very
helpful in my learning

00:40:18.880 --> 00:40:21.930
curve being super accelerated.

00:40:21.930 --> 00:40:24.210
And here I am today.

00:40:24.210 --> 00:40:28.620
So honestly, I wish I could
tell you that I grew up dreaming

00:40:28.620 --> 00:40:31.320
of being a data
scientist, but I didn't.

00:40:31.320 --> 00:40:32.040
I really didn't.

00:40:32.040 --> 00:40:35.050
I just ended up here and
when I was here I was like,

00:40:35.050 --> 00:40:38.275
whoa, this is amazing,
I'm going to stay.

00:40:38.275 --> 00:40:40.650
I don't think anybody wants
to be a data scientist when

00:40:40.650 --> 00:40:41.930
they're a kid.

00:40:41.930 --> 00:40:42.430
Yeah.

00:40:42.430 --> 00:40:45.150
Everybody, we're
taught to hate math.

00:40:45.150 --> 00:40:47.830
But I'll tell you one thing,
I loved math my whole life.

00:40:47.830 --> 00:40:51.090
So that's one of the reasons
engineering was the path for me

00:40:51.090 --> 00:40:53.190
because math was my
favorite subject in school.

00:40:53.190 --> 00:40:57.300
If I got a 99 out of 100
in math, I would be crying.

00:40:57.300 --> 00:41:02.040
Going back to me
being hard on myself.

00:41:02.040 --> 00:41:04.140
My love for my
somehow led me here.

00:41:04.140 --> 00:41:08.500
But it was not planned at all.

00:41:08.500 --> 00:41:11.380
Now I'm starting to
think what did we all

00:41:11.380 --> 00:41:13.900
want to be when we
were little and how it

00:41:13.900 --> 00:41:16.600
got to what we are doing now?

00:41:16.600 --> 00:41:19.390
I was just thinking Marlene,
when you say you wanted to

00:41:19.390 --> 00:41:22.630
or you were talking
about being a sleuth

00:41:22.630 --> 00:41:24.760
and finding things in the data.

00:41:24.760 --> 00:41:28.480
I can remember like
wanting to be a detective.

00:41:28.480 --> 00:41:31.300
and it's kind of the same
thing with data, too.

00:41:31.300 --> 00:41:33.160
I wanted to be
a vet when I was

00:41:33.160 --> 00:41:35.090
a kid because I love animals.

00:41:35.090 --> 00:41:38.440
But when I was like 10, I
realized that every time I

00:41:38.440 --> 00:41:40.360
see blood, I pass out.

00:41:40.360 --> 00:41:42.250
So that was a no.

00:41:42.250 --> 00:41:44.860
But yeah, I never
thought of the data world

00:41:44.860 --> 00:41:48.060
until I really got into it.

00:41:48.060 --> 00:41:50.810
I see there was an anonymous
question a bit earlier.

00:41:50.810 --> 00:41:55.250
And it was, how do you handle
managing the hidden metadata

00:41:55.250 --> 00:41:58.550
that is based more in
historical knowledge versus well

00:41:58.550 --> 00:42:00.550
documented?

00:42:00.550 --> 00:42:04.030
Yeah we struggled
with that a lot.

00:42:04.030 --> 00:42:07.240
Because we didn't have
a lot of documentation.

00:42:07.240 --> 00:42:09.070
We still don't
have documentation

00:42:09.070 --> 00:42:11.560
for a lot of historical data.

00:42:11.560 --> 00:42:15.310
But what I started
doing, and this

00:42:15.310 --> 00:42:18.140
has been since I joined
the organization.

00:42:18.140 --> 00:42:21.100
I'm just the, I just
can't remember stuff.

00:42:21.100 --> 00:42:22.880
I hate having to remember stuff.

00:42:22.880 --> 00:42:24.670
So I write everything down.

00:42:24.670 --> 00:42:27.340
Like one node and
Google Keep and those

00:42:27.340 --> 00:42:28.540
are like my best friends.

00:42:28.540 --> 00:42:31.810
So I write everything
down because I

00:42:31.810 --> 00:42:33.340
have trouble remembering stuff.

00:42:33.340 --> 00:42:37.300
And the way I look at it is
if your computer is your,

00:42:37.300 --> 00:42:40.480
I mean if your brain
is your computer,

00:42:40.480 --> 00:42:43.870
why would you want to waste
your hard drive with information

00:42:43.870 --> 00:42:44.980
you don't need to, right?

00:42:44.980 --> 00:42:46.230
You don't need to keep things.

00:42:46.230 --> 00:42:49.270
So what I like to do is I just
put all that in my notebook

00:42:49.270 --> 00:42:52.000
so that my mind is free to
actually focus on things

00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:53.620
and do some thinking.

00:42:53.620 --> 00:42:56.470
So when I joined,
this is something

00:42:56.470 --> 00:42:59.090
I learned quickly that we didn't
have a lot of documentation.

00:42:59.090 --> 00:43:01.090
So I just started
asking questions.

00:43:01.090 --> 00:43:04.000
Again, I have a
good mix on my team.

00:43:04.000 --> 00:43:06.550
I have people on my team who've
been with the organization

00:43:06.550 --> 00:43:08.740
for many, many, many years.

00:43:08.740 --> 00:43:10.990
I have people across the
organization who have

00:43:10.990 --> 00:43:12.880
been with us for many years.

00:43:12.880 --> 00:43:15.190
So I just started asking.

00:43:15.190 --> 00:43:17.177
I just started asking
a lot of questions.

00:43:17.177 --> 00:43:18.010
And I still do that.

00:43:18.010 --> 00:43:21.280
Every time I'm given a project
I don't know much about it

00:43:21.280 --> 00:43:22.840
because I don't
have the history,

00:43:22.840 --> 00:43:27.040
I'm just going to ask the
stupidest possible questions.

00:43:27.040 --> 00:43:29.050
Because no question
is too stupid.

00:43:29.050 --> 00:43:31.270
So one thing you
have to do is not

00:43:31.270 --> 00:43:34.120
be afraid of coming
across as stupid.

00:43:34.120 --> 00:43:35.860
Just ask questions.

00:43:35.860 --> 00:43:37.660
And write down those answers.

00:43:37.660 --> 00:43:41.510
My notebook became my
source for everything.

00:43:41.510 --> 00:43:44.340
When I became the
leader of the team,

00:43:44.340 --> 00:43:46.590
I realized, you know what?

00:43:46.590 --> 00:43:48.665
Everybody on the team
could benefit from this.

00:43:48.665 --> 00:43:50.040
And that's when
I started posting

00:43:50.040 --> 00:43:51.630
this on our shared folder.

00:43:51.630 --> 00:43:54.030
And today we have
data dictionaries

00:43:54.030 --> 00:44:00.800
I want to say for 80%
of our key data sets.

00:44:00.800 --> 00:44:03.980
We have data dictionaries to
help us out with the metadata.

00:44:03.980 --> 00:44:05.810
So it was just something
I started putting.

00:44:05.810 --> 00:44:08.870
And now, when we have
new data sets come in,

00:44:08.870 --> 00:44:12.290
we ask for the data
dictionary upfront.

00:44:12.290 --> 00:44:15.050
We ask that their data
dictionary be created upfront

00:44:15.050 --> 00:44:17.090
so that in the future
no one has to do

00:44:17.090 --> 00:44:20.130
what we had to do to get here.

00:44:20.130 --> 00:44:23.430
So we just started by asking
questions, putting it together

00:44:23.430 --> 00:44:25.200
and now we have something.

00:44:28.720 --> 00:44:30.910
Katherine, I see you
have your hand raised?

00:44:30.910 --> 00:44:31.450
Yeah.

00:44:31.450 --> 00:44:34.510
Just following up on
that last statement.

00:44:34.510 --> 00:44:37.900
At what point do you decide
as a data science team

00:44:37.900 --> 00:44:40.420
that it's not your
team's responsibility

00:44:40.420 --> 00:44:43.540
to be producing those
documentations and those data

00:44:43.540 --> 00:44:45.130
dictionaries?

00:44:45.130 --> 00:44:48.340
And where, is that your
team grows enough that you

00:44:48.340 --> 00:44:50.110
can split off like a baby team?

00:44:50.110 --> 00:44:52.810
Or is that something
that you find

00:44:52.810 --> 00:44:55.030
yourself needing that
you shouldn't be doing

00:44:55.030 --> 00:44:58.000
that sort of data grunt work?

00:44:58.000 --> 00:45:00.130
Exactly.

00:45:00.130 --> 00:45:04.240
That is always going to be,
that's an ongoing battle,

00:45:04.240 --> 00:45:05.710
right?

00:45:05.710 --> 00:45:09.160
There's always kind of
an ambiguity on hey,

00:45:09.160 --> 00:45:12.760
does this fall on the software
side, the technology side

00:45:12.760 --> 00:45:14.290
of the data engineering team?

00:45:14.290 --> 00:45:17.350
Or is this the BIA, which is
writing Business Information

00:45:17.350 --> 00:45:18.160
Analysis?

00:45:18.160 --> 00:45:20.380
Is this on BIA, or is
this on technology?

00:45:20.380 --> 00:45:23.560
That's an ongoing battle.

00:45:23.560 --> 00:45:26.290
Because we didn't have anything.

00:45:26.290 --> 00:45:28.300
I just needed, we
needed something.

00:45:28.300 --> 00:45:31.180
We took on the responsibility,
did it ourselves.

00:45:31.180 --> 00:45:34.390
We got good, we have peers
on the data engineering side

00:45:34.390 --> 00:45:36.860
and technology who
are very helpful.

00:45:36.860 --> 00:45:39.130
But of course, technology
has other priorities.

00:45:39.130 --> 00:45:41.890
They support technology
in the store.

00:45:41.890 --> 00:45:45.130
Their priority is to make
sure that systems in the store

00:45:45.130 --> 00:45:47.350
are working as opposed
to, hey, does BIA

00:45:47.350 --> 00:45:50.450
have the data
dictionary they need?

00:45:50.450 --> 00:45:54.910
So it's just consciously
realizing that sometimes you

00:45:54.910 --> 00:45:55.750
have to say no.

00:45:55.750 --> 00:46:00.310
Another good book I would
recommend, Not My Monkey.

00:46:00.310 --> 00:46:02.440
That's something
that really helped

00:46:02.440 --> 00:46:05.950
me focus on, OK,
this is something

00:46:05.950 --> 00:46:08.320
that we could be doing.

00:46:08.320 --> 00:46:10.450
But should we really
be doing this?

00:46:10.450 --> 00:46:13.930
Just because my team has the
skills to support something,

00:46:13.930 --> 00:46:16.130
does it really fall on my team?

00:46:16.130 --> 00:46:21.490
So I just, you have to
kind of politely start

00:46:21.490 --> 00:46:25.030
to ignore the things
and start holding people

00:46:25.030 --> 00:46:27.550
accountable for what
they're supposed to do.

00:46:27.550 --> 00:46:28.780
Other things.

00:46:28.780 --> 00:46:30.400
Do you always succeed?

00:46:30.400 --> 00:46:34.040
Maybe not, but that's a start.

00:46:34.040 --> 00:46:35.290
The other thing.

00:46:35.290 --> 00:46:37.930
The way I differentiate
a project that's

00:46:37.930 --> 00:46:40.930
for our technology
group versus my group

00:46:40.930 --> 00:46:48.130
is I tell the project
owner OK, are you

00:46:48.130 --> 00:46:52.450
trying to build a tool
or an artifact that's

00:46:52.450 --> 00:46:54.640
going to support the business?

00:46:54.640 --> 00:46:58.450
Or are you trying to identify
ways to improve your business

00:46:58.450 --> 00:47:01.270
processes using data?

00:47:01.270 --> 00:47:01.770
Right?

00:47:01.770 --> 00:47:04.650
Because a lot of times they
will come to us for tools.

00:47:04.650 --> 00:47:08.070
He built as a tool where we
can enter this information

00:47:08.070 --> 00:47:10.660
and collect data
in the database.

00:47:10.660 --> 00:47:12.600
But that's technology.

00:47:12.600 --> 00:47:15.540
So basically just
defining the difference

00:47:15.540 --> 00:47:18.750
between what my team does and
what the technology team does

00:47:18.750 --> 00:47:20.190
has helped us come a long way.

00:47:20.190 --> 00:47:22.290
Of course, you have
to get buy ins.

00:47:22.290 --> 00:47:26.100
So my peer on the technology
side, we work collaboratively.

00:47:26.100 --> 00:47:28.260
So a lot of it comes
from collaboration.

00:47:28.260 --> 00:47:32.790
We put together kind of a
map for the organization.

00:47:32.790 --> 00:47:37.770
Like, OK, if you want to do xyz,
the database team can help you.

00:47:37.770 --> 00:47:41.590
If you want abc, the
BIA team can help you.

00:47:41.590 --> 00:47:45.007
And it's a lot of just
answering the same questions

00:47:45.007 --> 00:47:45.840
over and over again.

00:47:45.840 --> 00:47:47.490
Even though we have
all this documentation,

00:47:47.490 --> 00:47:49.200
people are still going
to come asking for stuff

00:47:49.200 --> 00:47:51.150
that probably BI is not
supposed to be doing.

00:47:51.150 --> 00:47:52.930
Technology is supposed to do it.

00:47:52.930 --> 00:47:54.900
You just have to keep
reminding people.

00:47:54.900 --> 00:47:57.300
And eventually, it catches on.

00:48:00.520 --> 00:48:01.020
Awesome.

00:48:01.020 --> 00:48:02.490
Thank you.

00:48:02.490 --> 00:48:04.260
Sure.

00:48:04.260 --> 00:48:06.210
Namrata, I like
the perspective

00:48:06.210 --> 00:48:08.490
you shared on failure.

00:48:08.490 --> 00:48:13.110
And I'm curious, is there like
a certain failure or something

00:48:13.110 --> 00:48:16.800
that you have in mind that
you really learned from?

00:48:16.800 --> 00:48:20.160
The failure that
I learned from.

00:48:20.160 --> 00:48:22.330
I learned from my
all my failures,

00:48:22.330 --> 00:48:24.450
I'm trying to
think of which one.

00:48:24.450 --> 00:48:27.300
Because I have so many.

00:48:27.300 --> 00:48:27.810
Let's see.

00:48:30.510 --> 00:48:35.010
I think my, I think for me,
because the people aspect

00:48:35.010 --> 00:48:36.600
of things was always
stressful for me.

00:48:36.600 --> 00:48:38.820
The data aspect, it's fun.

00:48:38.820 --> 00:48:40.620
I can do it.

00:48:40.620 --> 00:48:42.810
When I'm talking
to people I think

00:48:42.810 --> 00:48:48.180
one thing that I learned from my
failures was reading the room.

00:48:48.180 --> 00:48:51.780
A big thing I've learned
is the data story

00:48:51.780 --> 00:48:56.330
that I'm telling it's going to
have many different versions.

00:48:56.330 --> 00:48:58.380
It's going to tell
the same story.

00:48:58.380 --> 00:49:01.520
But it's going to have many
different versions, depending

00:49:01.520 --> 00:49:03.380
on who I'm talking to.

00:49:03.380 --> 00:49:09.080
If I'm talking to a district
manager or a senior vice

00:49:09.080 --> 00:49:13.580
president person on UPS
or the UPS store site,

